WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.427
The second CTE parses dates.

00:00:02.427 --> 00:00:04.122
It recognizes two documented formats.

00:00:04.122 --> 00:00:09.927
If the raw text matches one of them, PostgreSQL creates a typed date.

00:00:09.927 --> 00:00:15.752
If the value does not match a supported format, the clean date becomes NULL.

00:00:15.752 --> 00:00:20.707
That preserves the distinction between known and unsupported input.

00:00:20.707 --> 00:00:27.552
Connect this idea to a customer master-data project cleaning duplicate profiles.

00:00:27.552 --> 00:00:32.117
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:32.117 --> 00:00:39.687
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:39.687 --> 00:00:45.977
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:45.977 --> 00:00:51.600
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
